---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Bars from "../components/Bars.astro";
import { agent, metrics, runs, setup } from "../data/numbers";
import { REPO } from "../lib/release";

const apps = [...new Set(runs.map((r) => r.app))];
---

<Base
  title="The numbers — Orbit"
  description="Orbit against Zed, VS Code and Cursor: size on disk, time to the first window, memory, processes and idle CPU."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="page__head">
      <div class="lead">
        <h1><b>The numbers.</b></h1> Orbit against Zed, VS Code and Cursor, measured on one Mac on {setup.date}: {`${setup.mac}, ${setup.macos}`}. Each app opened the same small TypeScript project ({setup.project}), three times after a
        warm-up launch; the bars are medians.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="numbers">
      <Bars metric={metrics.disk} icon="box" />
      <Bars metric={metrics.launch} icon="bolt" />
      <Bars metric={metrics.memory} icon="memory" />
      <Bars metric={metrics.processes} icon="stack" />
      <Bars metric={metrics.idle} icon="gauge" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose method">
      <h2 id="agents">Agents aren't counted</h2>
      <p>
        An agent is its own program — Claude Code and Codex are CLIs Orbit starts and talks to over ACP — and it costs
        the same in any editor that runs it. An Orbit window opens with a chat, so the agent you used last starts with
        it; the numbers above leave it and everything it starts out. With it, on the same runs: <strong
          >{agent.memory} MB in {agent.processes} processes</strong
        >, of which Orbit's own share is the {metrics.memory.rows[0]?.value} MB above.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cursor and VS Code ran with fresh profiles and no extensions, Zed with a fresh profile too: each as light as it
        gets. Orbit ran as it's installed here, with its settings, extensions and language servers.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how">How</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Size on disk</strong> is the app bundle, as Finder counts it.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>First window</strong> is the time from <code>open -a</code> to the first window of at least 400 × 300 on
          screen, polled every 5 ms. The app wasn't running, but its files were in the disk cache — what you get
          opening it a second time in a day, not after a restart.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Memory</strong> is the sum of <code>footprint</code> — Activity Monitor's “Memory” — over every process
          the app owns, 15 seconds after the window appears: those from its bundle, and everything they start
          (helpers, language servers, the terminal's shell, git).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Processes</strong> counts the same set.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Idle CPU</strong> is the CPU time all of them used in the next 30 seconds, with nobody touching anything.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The tool is a hundred lines of Swift; it and the project are in{" "}
        <a href={`${REPO}/tree/master/site/bench`}>site/bench</a>. Run it on your Mac — the numbers will differ, the
        proportions shouldn't much.
      </p>

      <h2 id="runs">Every run</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>App</th>
            <th>Run</th>
            <th>First window</th>
            <th>Memory</th>
            <th>Processes</th>
            <th>Idle CPU</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            runs.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.app}</td>
                <td>{r.run}</td>
                <td>{r.launch} ms</td>
                <td>{r.memory} MB</td>
                <td>{r.processes}</td>
                <td>{r.cpu.toFixed(2)} s</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Versions: {apps.map((a) => `${a} ${setup.versions[a] ?? ""}`).join(", ")}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .numbers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 56px 64px;
    margin-bottom: 96px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .numbers { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .method td { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
